<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James II of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1633">1633</a>–<a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as James VII of Scotland, James, Duke of York</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, also James VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, was King of England, Scotland and Ireland from 1685 to 1688. As Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> he received New Netherland from his brother Charles II in 1664 and it was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; as king he created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and absolutism led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a>, and his defeat in Ireland in 1690 ended his hopes of return.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="James II of England" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Peter Lely · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_II_by_Peter_Lely.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James was born on 14 October 1633 at St James's Palace, the second surviving son of Charles I and Henrietta Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and was designated Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a> at birth. At the age of three he was appointed Lord High Admiral, an honorary post that became a substantive office after the Restoration. He was present at the Battle of Edgehill in October 1642 and spent most of the next four years in the Royalist capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Restoration of 1660</a> he was created Duke of Albany in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and was confirmed as Lord High Admiral. In October 1660 Charles II made him Governor of the Royal Adventurers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the company later reformed as the Royal African Company, which over its lifetime carried more enslaved Africans across the Atlantic than any other single institution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Exclusion Crisis of 1679–81, when the Whigs tried to bar him from the succession, failed because the memory of the Commonwealth made the public reluctant to undermine hereditary succession. Charles II died on 6 February 1685, and James succeeded with widespread support, many believing that a Catholic monarchy would be temporary. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 23 April 1685.</p><p>He enlarged the standing army and used his dispensing power to let Roman Catholics command regiments without taking the oath required by the Test Act. When Parliament objected he prorogued it in November 1685, and it never met again in his reign. In April 1688 he re-issued the Declaration of Indulgence and ordered the clergy to read it. Seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who petitioned against it were tried for seditious libel and acquitted on 30 June, and the birth of his son James Francis Edward on 10 June raised the prospect of a Catholic dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leading English figures invited William of Orange, James's nephew and son-in-law, who landed at Brixham on 5 November 1688. James's army deserted and he went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 23 December. In February 1689 a Convention Parliament held that he had vacated the throne and installed William and Mary. James landed in Ireland in March 1689 with French help, but William defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-boyne">Battle of the Boyne</a> on 1 July 1690 (Old Style), and James fled to France, never to return. He lived at Saint-Germain-en-Laye, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s protection, until his death on 16 September 1701.</p><p>His son was recognised as James III and VIII by his supporters, later called Jacobites, and led a rising in 1715. Historians have disagreed on James: Macaulay cast him as a cruel absolutist, while some 20th-century writers praised his advocacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>, and more recent scholarship takes a middle course.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James II of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…refuge on the island in 1646 and again in 1649–1650, and he was proclaimed king in Saint Helier on 17 February 1649; after the Restoration his brother <b>James, Duke of York</b>, granted Sir George Carteret and Lord Berkeley lands in America that were named New Jersey in 1664.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Williamite War of 1689–1691 the Protestant city of Derry withstood a siege of 105 days, William III defeated <b>James II</b> at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and the Battle of Aughrim in 1691 preceded the Treaty of Limerick.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Restoration of 1660, <b>James II of England</b>, a Catholic, prorogued Parliament in 1685 and used his prerogative to suspend the laws against Catholics and Protestant dissenters.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles II died at Whitehall and his Catholic brother succeeded as <b>James II of England</b> and VII of Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-6">6 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>While Duke Jacob was held prisoner by the Swedes, the Dutch seized the fort in 1659, and in 1661 the English took it and renamed it James Island after the <b>Duke of York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a></li><li><blockquote>After the English conquest of 1664 the area was ruled from New York under <b>James, Duke of York</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Saint-Germain-en-Laye, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1677" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James II of England"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1677">What was here in 1677? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
